From 5c45db6bef17823b952f6cca42a149a669c48d90 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexanderNyr <alexander@auralite.local>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 20:20:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] X3 contract lifecycle: deploy, code hash, version, upgrade,
 migrate

A contract becomes a record in state instead of a byte string handed to the
executor:

* aura_exec::lifecycle -- CodeRecord (magic "ALCRYCD1" || format || abi ||
  version || len || code, strictly decoded), Lifecycle::{deploy, upgrade,
  call_stored}, LifecycleReceipt/LifecycleStatus, and LifecycleError with a
  stable code() string per refusal.
* Account::code_hash = BLAKE3(code) is the identity of the logic, so an account
  without code is Hash::ZERO and 'is this a contract?' needs no side table.
* Deploy is atomic with instantiation: one session stores the code, funds the
  account and runs the optional init -- a trapping or out-of-gas init leaves no
  account, no code and no moved value.
* Upgrade is optimistic and explicit (expected_code_version) and rewrites only
  the code record and code_hash: balance and storage survive, migration is
  contract logic via migrate(from, to).
* Code is priced, not capped: 1000 + 10/byte (=> ~100 KiB per transaction at
  the Stage 1 budget).
* ModuleLimits::hosted() enforces the X0 census at deploy while allowing
  exactly the seven X2 ABI imports; Engine::entry_arity resolves init/migrate
  before any state is touched.

Gates: 221 tests (12 new lifecycle, 1 doctest), 4595 vectors across 21
fixtures, 129 negative controls (6 new), fmt/clippy clean, dependency gate
unchanged (239 external, no new workspace edge). Measured gas: deploy 3560,
call 30, upgrade 4527, call-after-upgrade 30, run-only deploy 2860.

Defects found by the gates and fixed here: the X0 import ban made the X2 ABI
unloadable (ModuleLimits::allow_abi_imports), entry_arity could not go through
prepare (it requires a run export), a deploy without init was reported as a
trap, and LifecycleError::code() collapsed every refusal into refused:censored.

Honest scope: hosted execution is still wasmi-only; deploy/upgrade are explicit
but not permissioned (no caller == owner -- signatures are T-phase); no code
archive, no factory/proxy, no cross-contract calls.
